  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem requires finding a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral, which is equivalent to minimizing the maximum correlation between h and 1-h. The key bottlenecks in the previous approach are limited exploration of the search space and reliance on gradient-based methods that can get trapped in local optima. To improve, we adopt a multi-stage optimization strategy combining global exploration with local refinement, using a more structured initialization based on periodic patterns. This approach balances computational efficiency with the ability to escape local minima effectively.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise constant function with n_points samples over [0, 2], satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] * dx = 1 (i.e., \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2)\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC_5 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\nThis is equivalent to:\nC_5 = max_lag (np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nConstraints:\n- h \u2208 [0, 1]\u207f_points\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nWe use the scipy.optimize.minimize function with:\n- L-BFGS-B for local refinement\n- A global search with random perturbations for exploration\n- A penalty method to enforce the \u2211h constraint\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- If initial_h_values exists, use it directly (verify \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2)\n- Else, create a periodic step pattern with period T dividing [0, 2]\n- Generate h[i] = 1 if i mod m == r, else 0 where m = n_points / 2, r randomly chosen\n- Rescale h to satisfy \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nStage 2: Global search with random perturbations\n- Start with the initial guess\n- For 200 iterations:\n   - Apply a random perturbation (\u00b10.1) to all elements\n   - Clip to [0, 1]\n   - Normalize to satisfy \u2211h = n_points / 2\n   - Evaluate C_5\n\nStage 3: Local refinement with L-BFGS-B\n- Use the best candidate from Stage 2 as initial guess\n- Perform 500 iterations of L-BFGS-B optimization\n   - Objective: -C_5 (minimize negative to maximize)\n   - Bounds: [0, 1] for all elements\n   - Constraint: \u2211h = n_points / 2 (equality constraint)\n- Track the best solution during optimization\n\nStage 4: Final evaluation\n- Re-evaluate the best solution using the exact correlation function\n- Return the vector with C_5 bound and n_points\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 500 (balances resolution and computation time)\n- Penalization factor = 1000 for \u2211h constraint\n- Perturbation amplitude = 0.1\n- Initial random seed = 42\n\nVariations:\n- If initial_h_values exists, apply a sinusoidal perturbation to it\n- For n_points < 200, double the resolution and downsample\n- If convergence stalls, switch to differential evolution for exploration\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 200s for global search with perturbations\n- 300s for local refinement with L-BFGS-B\n- 100s for final evaluation and fallback\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Clamp all values to [0, 1] after each update\n- If \u2211h deviates by >1e-6, apply a rescaling factor\n- Track the top 5 candidates and re-evaluate them if optimization stalls\n- Use early stopping if C_5 improves by <1e-5 over 10 iterations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1]\n- Confirm \u2211h == n_points / 2 (within 1e-6)\n- Ensure C_5 is a finite positive number\n\nFinal validation:\n- Re-evaluate the candidate using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh minimize call\n- Output the list of h_values, C_5 bound, and n_points\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm no NaNs or infinities in the result\n- Validate that the correlation calculation matches the evaluator\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values",
